---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { withBase } from '../../utils/paths';
import { papers } from '../../data/site';

const paper = papers[0];
---

<Layout
	title="When did the market care about ESG? — Vladislav Pyzhov"
	description="A visual, plain-English guide to detecting ESG news and measuring how the stock market's response changed over time."
>
	<article class="story page-shell">
		<header class="story-header rule">
			<a class="back-link" href={withBase('/research/')}>← Research index</a>
			<div class="story-meta">
				<p class="eyebrow">Visual explainer · Paper 01</p>
				<p>About 10 minutes · no finance background needed</p>
			</div>
			<h1>The market changed its mind about ESG.</h1>
			<p class="dek">We followed ESG news from the early 2000s onward. At first, even good news could look like an expensive distraction. Then it became valuable. More recently, the reward has faded again.</p>
			<div class="btn-row">
				<a class="btn primary" href="#story">Start the story <span>↓</span></a>
				<a class="btn" href={paper.url} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Full paper <span>↗</span></a>
			</div>
		</header>

		<figure class="opening-visual" aria-labelledby="opening-title opening-caption">
			<div class="figure-topline">
				<p id="opening-title">The central result</p>
				<p>Market response to positive ESG news · over time</p>
			</div>
			<svg viewBox="0 0 1120 560" role="img" aria-label="An inverted U curve: the response to positive ESG news begins below zero, rises above zero, then falls back towards zero.">
				<defs>
					<linearGradient id="curve-gradient" x1="0" x2="1">
						<stop offset="0" stop-color="#ff6b45" />
						<stop offset=".5" stop-color="#7867ff" />
						<stop offset="1" stop-color="#188f76" />
					</linearGradient>
				</defs>
				<g class="opening-grid">
					<line x1="90" y1="100" x2="90" y2="470" />
					<line x1="90" y1="350" x2="1050" y2="350" class="zero" />
					<line x1="90" y1="470" x2="1050" y2="470" />
					<line x1="360" y1="100" x2="360" y2="470" />
					<line x1="760" y1="100" x2="760" y2="470" />
				</g>
				<text x="25" y="120" class="axis-label">REWARD</text>
				<text x="25" y="445" class="axis-label">PENALTY</text>
				<text x="108" y="510" class="era-label">EARLY 2000s</text>
				<text x="430" y="510" class="era-label">LATE 2000s — MID 2010s</text>
				<text x="810" y="510" class="era-label">LATE 2010s →</text>
				<path class="opening-curve-shadow" d="M110 405 C260 380 300 210 435 145 C575 78 705 119 790 235 C858 328 932 333 1030 340" />
				<path class="opening-curve" pathLength="1" d="M110 405 C260 380 300 210 435 145 C575 78 705 119 790 235 C858 328 932 333 1030 340" />
				<g class="curve-callout early"><circle cx="150" cy="390" r="8" /><text x="170" y="384">GOOD NEWS</text><text x="170" y="408">CAN LOOK COSTLY</text></g>
				<g class="curve-callout middle"><circle cx="565" cy="114" r="8" /><text x="585" y="108">GOOD NEWS</text><text x="585" y="132">IS REWARDED</text></g>
				<g class="curve-callout recent"><circle cx="965" cy="332" r="8" /><text x="985" y="326">REWARD</text><text x="985" y="350">FADES</text></g>
			</svg>
			<figcaption id="opening-caption"><strong>A reverse U.</strong> The reward for positive ESG news rises, peaks, then weakens. Negative ESG news remains the harsher signal. The curve shows the paper’s qualitative pattern, not point estimates.</figcaption>
		</figure>

		<div id="story" class="prose story-prose">
			<p class="standfirst">That curve is the destination. But to understand it, we first need to solve a surprisingly difficult problem: <em>what counts as an ESG event?</em></p>
			<p>ESG is shorthand for how a company affects the environment, treats people and governs itself. Every day brings thousands of articles, posts and passing mentions. Most are background noise. We need to find the moments when the public story around one company changes sharply enough to matter.</p>
		</div>

		<section class="chapter detector" data-method aria-labelledby="detector-title">
			<div class="chapter-number"><span>01</span><p>Find the event</p></div>
			<div class="chapter-body">
				<header class="chapter-heading">
					<p class="eyebrow">The T-Mobile example</p>
					<h2 id="detector-title">A needle in a newsroom.</h2>
					<p>Between January 2014 and January 2016, T-Mobile had plenty of noisy media days. The algorithm keeps only the dates when <strong>attention</strong> and <strong>sentiment</strong> move unusually at the same time.</p>
				</header>

				<div class="method-player">
					<div class="method-controls" aria-label="Event detection animation steps">
						<button class="active" type="button" data-method-button="0"><span>1</span> Watch</button>
						<button type="button" data-method-button="1"><span>2</span> Compare</button>
						<button type="button" data-method-button="2"><span>3</span> Standardise</button>
						<button type="button" data-method-button="3"><span>4</span> Keep</button>
						<button class="player-toggle" type="button" data-method-toggle aria-label="Pause animation">Pause</button>
					</div>

					<div class="signal-card">
						<div class="signal-head">
							<p>T-Mobile · illustrative signal reconstruction</p>
							<p data-method-status>Start with the raw daily signals</p>
						</div>
						<svg viewBox="0 0 1100 590" role="img" aria-label="Two time series for T-Mobile, media attention and ESG sentiment, with three simultaneous shocks highlighted as events.">
							<g class="signal-grid">
								<line x1="105" y1="110" x2="1040" y2="110" /><line x1="105" y1="205" x2="1040" y2="205" /><line x1="105" y1="300" x2="1040" y2="300" />
								<line x1="105" y1="355" x2="1040" y2="355" /><line x1="105" y1="440" x2="1040" y2="440" /><line x1="105" y1="525" x2="1040" y2="525" />
							</g>
							<text x="25" y="194" class="signal-label">MEDIA</text><text x="25" y="216" class="signal-label">ATTENTION</text>
							<text x="25" y="430" class="signal-label">ESG</text><text x="25" y="452" class="signal-label">SENTIMENT</text>
							<text x="105" y="565" class="date-label">JAN 2014</text><text x="520" y="565" class="date-label">JAN 2015</text><text x="958" y="565" class="date-label">JAN 2016</text>
							<g class="thresholds"><line x1="105" y1="148" x2="1040" y2="148" /><text x="930" y="140">UNUSUAL</text><line x1="105" y1="397" x2="1040" y2="397" /><text x="930" y="389">UNUSUAL</text></g>
							<path class="signal buzz" pathLength="1" d="M105 270 L135 260 L165 272 L195 255 L225 269 L252 258 L274 115 L294 266 L330 250 L363 270 L400 254 L433 267 L465 242 L487 121 L509 263 L545 267 L580 248 L615 260 L650 242 L688 271 L722 249 L752 265 L783 110 L804 256 L842 266 L880 246 L916 264 L955 248 L995 263 L1040 252" />
							<path class="signal sentiment" pathLength="1" d="M105 442 L135 454 L165 438 L195 448 L225 441 L252 458 L274 515 L294 445 L330 454 L363 443 L400 451 L433 440 L465 457 L487 511 L509 446 L545 439 L580 452 L615 443 L650 458 L688 441 L722 451 L752 442 L783 519 L804 449 L842 441 L880 454 L916 445 L955 451 L995 441 L1040 448" />
							<g class="change-marks"><line x1="274" y1="115" x2="274" y2="270" /><line x1="487" y1="121" x2="487" y2="242" /><line x1="783" y1="110" x2="783" y2="265" /><line x1="274" y1="458" x2="274" y2="515" /><line x1="487" y1="457" x2="487" y2="511" /><line x1="783" y1="442" x2="783" y2="519" /></g>
							<g class="event-band e1"><rect x="257" y="76" width="34" height="469" /><text x="274" y="60">01 JUL 2014</text></g>
							<g class="event-band e2"><rect x="470" y="76" width="34" height="469" /><text x="487" y="60">19 DEC 2014</text></g>
							<g class="event-band e3"><rect x="766" y="76" width="34" height="469" /><text x="783" y="60">01 OCT 2015</text></g>
							<line class="scanner" x1="105" y1="76" x2="105" y2="545" />
						</svg>
						<div class="event-notes" aria-label="The three detected T-Mobile events">
							<p><span>01 Jul ’14</span>Accusations of bogus charges without customers’ consent</p>
							<p><span>19 Dec ’14</span>US government settlement over “cramming” charges</p>
							<p><span>01 Oct ’15</span>Personal data stolen from 15 million applicants</p>
						</div>
					</div>

					<div class="method-explainer" aria-live="polite">
						<div class="method-copy active" data-method-copy="0">
							<p class="mini-label">Step 1 · Watch</p><h3>Two signals, every day.</h3>
							<p><strong>Attention</strong> tells us how loudly the media is talking. <strong>Sentiment</strong> tells us whether the story becomes better or worse.</p>
							<code>X<sub>t</sub> = today’s observed signal</code>
						</div>
						<div class="method-copy" data-method-copy="1">
							<p class="mini-label">Step 2 · Compare</p><h3>Look for the jump.</h3>
							<p>A high level alone is not news. We ask how much today changed from yesterday.</p>
							<code>ΔX<sub>t</sub> = X<sub>t</sub> − X<sub>t−1</sub></code>
						</div>
						<div class="method-copy" data-method-copy="2">
							<p class="mini-label">Step 3 · Standardise</p><h3>Ask: unusual for whom?</h3>
							<p>A large firm is always in the news. So each jump is compared with that company’s own previous 90 days.</p>
							<code>z<sub>t</sub> = ΔX<sub>t</sub> ÷ σ(ΔX<sub>t−90:t−1</sub>)</code>
						</div>
						<div class="method-copy" data-method-copy="3">
							<p class="mini-label">Step 4 · Keep</p><h3>Make both alarms ring.</h3>
							<p>We keep a date only when attention and sentiment both jump, coverage is large enough, and no event was selected in the previous 20 days.</p>
							<code>event = buzz shock ∩ sentiment shock ∩ coverage ∩ quiet period</code>
						</div>
					</div>
				</div>
				<p class="figure-note">The lines above are an illustrative reconstruction, not the paper’s underlying daily data. The event dates and descriptions follow the T-Mobile example in the paper.</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="chapter returns" data-car aria-labelledby="returns-title">
			<div class="chapter-number"><span>02</span><p>Measure the reaction</p></div>
			<div class="chapter-body">
				<header class="chapter-heading">
					<p class="eyebrow">From price to surprise</p>
					<h2 id="returns-title">Did the stock do something unusual too?</h2>
					<p>A share price moves for all sorts of reasons. We estimate the return that broad market forces would normally explain, subtract it from the actual return, and add those daily surprises together. That running total is the <strong>cumulative abnormal return</strong>, or CAR.</p>
				</header>

				<div class="car-card">
					<div class="car-toolbar">
						<div class="mode-buttons" aria-label="Choose example news direction">
							<button class="active" type="button" data-car-mode="negative">Negative news</button>
							<button type="button" data-car-mode="positive">Positive news</button>
						</div>
						<button class="replay" type="button" data-car-replay>Replay <span>↻</span></button>
					</div>
					<div class="car-readout">
						<div><span>EVENT DAY</span><strong data-car-day>−10</strong></div>
						<p data-car-message>Before the event, there is no sustained surprise.</p>
					</div>
					<svg viewBox="0 0 1100 500" role="img" aria-label="An animation of cumulative abnormal return from day minus 10 to day plus 10, initially flat, moving sharply on day zero and persisting afterwards.">
						<g class="car-grid"><line x1="90" y1="100" x2="1040" y2="100" /><line x1="90" y1="245" x2="1040" y2="245" class="car-zero" /><line x1="90" y1="390" x2="1040" y2="390" /><line x1="90" y1="70" x2="90" y2="420" /><line x1="565" y1="70" x2="565" y2="420" class="event-line" /></g>
						<text x="25" y="105" class="car-axis">+1%</text><text x="48" y="250" class="car-axis">0</text><text x="25" y="395" class="car-axis">−1%</text>
						<text x="88" y="455" class="car-axis">−10</text><text x="555" y="455" class="car-axis">0</text><text x="1024" y="455" class="car-axis">+10</text><text x="575" y="88" class="event-text">NEWS BREAKS</text>
						<path class="expected-line" d="M90 245 L1040 245" />
						<polyline class="car-line" data-car-line points="" />
						<line class="car-playhead" data-car-playhead x1="90" y1="70" x2="90" y2="420" />
						<circle class="car-dot" data-car-dot cx="90" cy="245" r="9" />
					</svg>
					<div class="equation-strip">
						<p><span>Daily surprise</span><strong>AR<sub>t</sub> = actual return − expected return</strong></p>
						<p><span>Running effect</span><strong>CAR = AR<sub>−10</sub> + … + AR<sub>+10</sub></strong></p>
					</div>
				</div>
				<p class="figure-note">Illustrative path, designed to explain the calculation: flat before the event, a sharp adjustment around day 0, then persistence. It is not a plotted estimate from the paper.</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="chapter result" data-era-explorer aria-labelledby="result-title">
			<div class="chapter-number"><span>03</span><p>Zoom out</p></div>
			<div class="chapter-body">
				<header class="chapter-heading">
					<p class="eyebrow">From one event to a market pattern</p>
					<h2 id="result-title">Repeat. Date. Compare.</h2>
					<p>Now repeat that event study across the sample. Put each event in calendar time and ask whether the average price reaction is changing. The cloud is messy; the time trend is not.</p>
				</header>

				<div class="result-card">
					<div class="result-steps" aria-label="How the reverse U is constructed">
						<p><span>1</span>Detect each ESG event</p><i>→</i><p><span>2</span>Measure its CAR</p><i>→</i><p><span>3</span>Arrange events by year</p><i>→</i><p><span>4</span>Estimate the time pattern</p>
					</div>
					<svg class="result-plot" viewBox="0 0 1100 560" role="img" aria-label="A scatter of event responses and a smooth inverted U time pattern from the early 2000s to the late 2010s.">
						<g class="result-grid"><line x1="90" y1="95" x2="1050" y2="95" /><line x1="90" y1="305" x2="1050" y2="305" class="zero" /><line x1="90" y1="470" x2="1050" y2="470" /><line x1="90" y1="95" x2="90" y2="470" /></g>
						<text x="25" y="115" class="axis-label">REWARD</text><text x="25" y="445" class="axis-label">PENALTY</text>
						<g class="scatter">
							<circle cx="120" cy="382" r="5"/><circle cx="145" cy="342" r="5"/><circle cx="175" cy="410" r="5"/><circle cx="205" cy="330" r="5"/><circle cx="232" cy="365" r="5"/><circle cx="258" cy="290" r="5"/>
							<circle cx="295" cy="255" r="5"/><circle cx="330" cy="280" r="5"/><circle cx="365" cy="210" r="5"/><circle cx="402" cy="184" r="5"/><circle cx="442" cy="225" r="5"/><circle cx="480" cy="145" r="5"/>
							<circle cx="520" cy="178" r="5"/><circle cx="560" cy="123" r="5"/><circle cx="603" cy="162" r="5"/><circle cx="645" cy="200" r="5"/><circle cx="684" cy="172" r="5"/><circle cx="722" cy="236" r="5"/>
							<circle cx="760" cy="218" r="5"/><circle cx="800" cy="275" r="5"/><circle cx="838" cy="246" r="5"/><circle cx="877" cy="316" r="5"/><circle cx="920" cy="280" r="5"/><circle cx="960" cy="328" r="5"/><circle cx="1005" cy="298" r="5"/>
						</g>
						<path class="result-curve" pathLength="1" d="M110 382 C255 345 315 236 430 165 C560 84 684 143 770 230 C854 315 922 307 1030 310" />
						<g class="era-marker era-early selected" data-era-point="early"><circle cx="178" cy="354" r="12" /><text x="112" y="520">EARLY 2000s</text></g>
						<g class="era-marker era-middle" data-era-point="middle"><circle cx="562" cy="126" r="12" /><text x="450" y="520">LATE 2000s — MID 2010s</text></g>
						<g class="era-marker era-recent" data-era-point="recent"><circle cx="945" cy="306" r="12" /><text x="864" y="520">LATE 2010s →</text></g>
					</svg>

					<div class="era-explorer">
						<div class="era-controls" role="tablist" aria-label="Explore the three periods">
							<button class="active" type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="true" data-era="early">Early 2000s</button>
							<button type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="false" data-era="middle">Late 2000s—mid 2010s</button>
							<button type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="false" data-era="recent">Late 2010s onward</button>
						</div>
						<div class="era-copy" aria-live="polite">
							<p class="mini-label" data-era-kicker>Act I · scepticism</p>
							<h3 data-era-title>“Is this just an expensive distraction?”</h3>
							<p data-era-body>The market penalised both positive and negative ESG news. Even a well-intentioned initiative could be read as managers spending shareholders’ money on something with uncertain payoff.</p>
							<div class="reaction-pills"><span data-positive-pill>Positive news ↓</span><span data-negative-pill>Negative news ↓</span></div>
						</div>
					</div>
				</div>
				<p class="figure-note">Dots are illustrative and show why individual events are noisy; the smooth line represents the paper’s qualitative time pattern. See the paper for estimates, specifications and uncertainty.</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="plain-conclusion">
			<p class="eyebrow">The result in one breath</p>
			<blockquote>Positive ESG news went from penalty, to reward, to little reaction. Negative news kept its bite.</blockquote>
			<p>That is why studies using different decades can appear to disagree: they may each be describing a different part of the same curve.</p>
		</section>

		<section class="details-section rule" aria-labelledby="details-title">
			<div class="details-intro">
				<p class="eyebrow">For the curious</p>
				<h2 id="details-title">One layer deeper.</h2>
				<p>The story above is deliberately simple. Open these notes for the mechanics behind it.</p>
			</div>
			<div class="details-list">
				<details>
					<summary><span>01</span>What exactly triggers an event?</summary>
					<div><p>The baseline algorithm looks back 90 days. It requires an attention shock and a sentiment shock of at least three historical standard deviations, at least 10 underlying references, and a 20-day gap since the previous selected event for that company.</p><p class="formula">z<sub>i,t</sub> = ΔX<sub>i,t</sub> / σ<sub>90</sub>(ΔX<sub>i</sub>)</p></div>
				</details>
				<details>
					<summary><span>02</span>How is “normal” return estimated?</summary>
					<div><p>A Fama–French five-factor model estimates the part of a stock’s return associated with broad market, size, value, profitability and investment factors. The leftover is the abnormal return.</p><p class="formula">R<sub>i,t</sub> = α<sub>i</sub> + β<sub>i</sub>(M<sub>t</sub>−RF<sub>t</sub>) + γ<sub>i</sub>SMB<sub>t</sub> + θ<sub>i</sub>HML<sub>t</sub> + δ<sub>i</sub>CMA<sub>t</sub> + φ<sub>i</sub>RMW<sub>t</sub> + ε<sub>i,t</sub></p></div>
				</details>
				<details>
					<summary><span>03</span>How do daily surprises become CAR?</summary>
					<div><p>Subtract the estimated normal return from the observed return each day, then sum those abnormal returns across the event window.</p><p class="formula">AR<sub>i,t</sub> = R<sub>i,t</sub> − R̂<sub>i,t</sub><br />CAR<sub>i</sub>[τ<sub>1</sub>,τ<sub>2</sub>] = Σ AR<sub>i,t</sub></p></div>
				</details>
				<details>
					<summary><span>04</span>What else should I know?</summary>
					<div><p>The time pattern is robust across ESG pillars, industries and firm characteristics. The paper also finds that smaller firms respond more to positive news, while larger firms are more sensitive to negative news. Those tests—and the uncertainty around every estimate—are why the full paper is much longer than this explainer.</p></div>
				</details>
			</div>
		</section>

		<footer class="story-end">
			<div><p class="eyebrow">Working paper · {paper.year}</p><h2>{paper.title}</h2><p>{paper.authors}</p></div>
			<a class="btn primary" href={paper.url} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Read the full paper <span>↗</span></a>
		</footer>
	</article>
</Layout>

<script>
	const method = document.querySelector('[data-method]');
	let methodStep = 0;
	let methodPlaying = true;
	let methodTimer;
	const methodStatus = [
		'Start with the raw daily signals',
		'Measure each day’s change',
		'Compare the jump with the previous 90 days',
		'Keep only dates when every test passes',
	];

	function showMethodStep(nextStep) {
		if (!method) return;
		methodStep = nextStep;
		method.setAttribute('data-step', String(methodStep));
		method.querySelectorAll('[data-method-button]').forEach((button) => {
			const active = Number(button.getAttribute('data-method-button')) === methodStep;
			button.classList.toggle('active', active);
		});
		method.querySelectorAll('[data-method-copy]').forEach((copy) => {
			copy.classList.toggle('active', Number(copy.getAttribute('data-method-copy')) === methodStep);
		});
		const status = method.querySelector('[data-method-status]');
		if (status) status.textContent = methodStatus[methodStep];
	}

	function scheduleMethod() {
		window.clearInterval(methodTimer);
		if (!methodPlaying) return;
		methodTimer = window.setInterval(() => showMethodStep((methodStep + 1) % 4), 3400);
	}

	method?.querySelectorAll('[data-method-button]').forEach((button) => {
		button.addEventListener('click', () => {
			showMethodStep(Number(button.getAttribute('data-method-button')));
			methodPlaying = false;
			const toggle = method.querySelector('[data-method-toggle]');
			if (toggle) { toggle.textContent = 'Play'; toggle.setAttribute('aria-label', 'Play animation'); }
			scheduleMethod();
		});
	});

	method?.querySelector('[data-method-toggle]')?.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
		methodPlaying = !methodPlaying;
		const button = event.currentTarget;
		if (button instanceof HTMLButtonElement) {
			button.textContent = methodPlaying ? 'Pause' : 'Play';
			button.setAttribute('aria-label', methodPlaying ? 'Pause animation' : 'Play animation');
		}
		if (methodPlaying) showMethodStep((methodStep + 1) % 4);
		scheduleMethod();
	});

	if (!window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches) scheduleMethod();
	else { methodPlaying = false; showMethodStep(3); }

	const car = document.querySelector('[data-car]');
	const carSeries = {
		negative: [0.01, 0, 0.02, 0.01, -0.01, 0, -0.02, -0.01, 0, -0.02, -0.58, -0.82, -0.91, -0.9, -0.92, -0.94, -0.93, -0.95, -0.96, -0.95, -0.97],
		positive: [0, 0.01, -0.01, 0, 0.02, 0.01, 0, 0.02, 0.01, 0.02, 0.31, 0.52, 0.61, 0.64, 0.63, 0.66, 0.65, 0.67, 0.66, 0.68, 0.68],
	};
	let carMode = 'negative';
	let carFrame;

	function carPoint(value, index) {
		return { x: 90 + index * 47.5, y: 245 - value * 145 };
	}

	function renderCar(index) {
		if (!car) return;
		const series = carSeries[carMode];
		const visible = series.slice(0, index + 1);
		const points = visible.map((value, pointIndex) => {
			const point = carPoint(value, pointIndex);
			return `${point.x},${point.y}`;
		}).join(' ');
		const line = car.querySelector('[data-car-line]');
		if (line) line.setAttribute('points', points);
		const current = carPoint(series[index], index);
		const dot = car.querySelector('[data-car-dot]');
		const playhead = car.querySelector('[data-car-playhead]');
		if (dot) { dot.setAttribute('cx', String(current.x)); dot.setAttribute('cy', String(current.y)); }
		if (playhead) { playhead.setAttribute('x1', String(current.x)); playhead.setAttribute('x2', String(current.x)); }
		const day = index - 10;
		const dayReadout = car.querySelector('[data-car-day]');
		if (dayReadout) dayReadout.textContent = day > 0 ? `+${day}` : day === 0 ? '0' : `−${Math.abs(day)}`;
		const message = car.querySelector('[data-car-message]');
		if (message) {
			if (day < 0) message.textContent = 'Before the event, there is no sustained surprise.';
			else if (day === 0) message.textContent = carMode === 'negative' ? 'The news hits. The price drops beyond what the market model expected.' : 'The news hits. The price rises beyond what the market model expected.';
			else message.textContent = 'The gap remains: the market does not immediately give the move back.';
		}
	}

	function playCar() {
		window.cancelAnimationFrame(carFrame);
		if (window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches) { renderCar(20); return; }
		const start = performance.now();
		const draw = (time) => {
			const index = Math.min(20, Math.floor((time - start) / 235));
			renderCar(index);
			if (index < 20) carFrame = window.requestAnimationFrame(draw);
		};
		carFrame = window.requestAnimationFrame(draw);
	}

	car?.querySelectorAll('[data-car-mode]').forEach((button) => {
		button.addEventListener('click', () => {
			carMode = button.getAttribute('data-car-mode') || 'negative';
			car.classList.toggle('positive-mode', carMode === 'positive');
			car.querySelectorAll('[data-car-mode]').forEach((item) => item.classList.toggle('active', item === button));
			playCar();
		});
	});
	car?.querySelector('[data-car-replay]')?.addEventListener('click', playCar);
	playCar();

	const explorer = document.querySelector('[data-era-explorer]');
	const eraContent = {
		early: {
			kicker: 'Act I · scepticism',
			title: '“Is this just an expensive distraction?”',
			body: 'The market penalised both positive and negative ESG news. Even a well-intentioned initiative could be read as managers spending shareholders’ money on something with uncertain payoff.',
			positive: 'Positive news ↓', negative: 'Negative news ↓',
		},
		middle: {
			kicker: 'Act II · alignment',
			title: 'Good news is rewarded. Bad news is punished.',
			body: 'From the late 2000s through the middle of the 2010s, the market response lines up with the direction of the news. ESG information behaves like financially relevant information.',
			positive: 'Positive news ↑', negative: 'Negative news ↓',
		},
		recent: {
			kicker: 'Act III · asymmetry',
			title: 'The upside fades. The downside stays.',
			body: 'From the late 2010s onward, the positive response weakens towards zero. Negative ESG news still carries a pronounced penalty: avoiding harm matters more than announcing virtue.',
			positive: 'Positive news →', negative: 'Negative news ↓↓',
		},
	};

	explorer?.querySelectorAll('[data-era]').forEach((button) => {
		button.addEventListener('click', () => {
			const era = button.getAttribute('data-era');
			if (!era || !(era in eraContent)) return;
			const content = eraContent[era];
			explorer.querySelectorAll('[data-era]').forEach((item) => {
				item.classList.toggle('active', item === button);
				item.setAttribute('aria-selected', String(item === button));
			});
			explorer.querySelectorAll('[data-era-point]').forEach((point) => point.classList.toggle('selected', point.getAttribute('data-era-point') === era));
			const assignments = [
				['[data-era-kicker]', content.kicker], ['[data-era-title]', content.title], ['[data-era-body]', content.body],
				['[data-positive-pill]', content.positive], ['[data-negative-pill]', content.negative],
			];
			assignments.forEach(([selector, value]) => { const target = explorer.querySelector(selector); if (target) target.textContent = value; });
		});
	});
